          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Eugène Delacroix's Early Life</strong><br>\nEugène Delacroix was born in 1798 near Paris, France. He was educated at the Lycée Louis-le-Grand and later attended the Parisian art school École des Beaux-Arts, beginning his career in the Romantic art movement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Massacre at Chios\"</strong><br>\nIn 1824, Delacroix's painting \"The Massacre at Chios\" was exhibited, revealing his dramatic and colorful style. The work depicted a recent atrocity committed by Ottoman forces, capturing the emotions of the victims with intense realism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Romanticism and Delacroix</strong><br>\nDelacroix became a leading figure of Romanticism, an artistic movement that emphasized emotion and individualism. His works often reflected these themes, exploring historical and contemporary events through a lens of dramatic expression.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
