          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Delacroix's Moroccan Influence</strong><br>\nAfter traveling to Morocco, Delacroix's style evolved to include brighter colors and a fascination with light. His North African experiences influenced many paintings, introducing European audiences to scenes of Eastern life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Liberty Leading the People\"</strong><br>\nPerhaps Delacroix's most renowned work, \"Liberty Leading the People\" (1830), symbolizes the spirit of revolution. It portrays the allegorical figure of Liberty and commemorates the July Revolution in France.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Techniques and Artistic Contributions</strong><br>\nDelacroix was known for his expressive brushstrokes and study of optical effects of color. This revolutionary use of color paved the way for the Impressionist and Post-Impressionist movements.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
