            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Inspirations</strong><br>\nDelacroix drew inspiration from literature, as evidenced by his illustrations for works by William Shakespeare, Walter Scott, and Johann Wolfgang von Goethe. His visual artistry complemented his passion for the written word.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Commissions and Final Years</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Delacroix received numerous public commissions, which included decorating government buildings. His later years were marked by a focus on murals and large-scale works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nDelacroix died in 1863, but his legacy lives on through his influential style and contributions to romantic art. His work continues to be celebrated for its emotional power and innovation in color and technique.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
